            <ArticleTitle>Lactotripeptides supplementations alleviate the decrease in maximal isometric force following high-intensity eccentric exercise: a randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blinded clinical trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PHM.0000000000000867</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate whether lactotripeptides (LTP) supplementations alleviate the decrease in the maximal isometric force (MIF), an indirect marker of muscle damage, following eccentric exercise (ECC).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-two young men performed 50-ECC of the elbow flexors using an isokinetic dynamometer. The subjects were randomly assigned to either the placebo or LTP group, and were each given a 4.5 mg/day placebo or LTP three-time on the exercise day and the day after. MIF and brachial arterial diameter were assessed before and two days after the ECC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The interaction of time and group on MIF was significant (p &lt; 0.05); MIF was significantly decreased in both groups following ECC (p &lt; 0.005). The interaction of brachial arterial diameter was significant (p &lt; 0.05); brachial arterial diameter was significantly increased in only the LTP group (p &lt; 0.005). In addition, the change in MIF was significantly related to the change in brachial arterial diameter after adjusting for body weight and change in ROM (p &lt; 0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The present results suggest that LTP supplementation alleviates the decrease in the MIF via an increase in brachial arterial diameter following ECC.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
